Z||000000||GT Bastard||02-09-2000||08:50 PM||||If anybody reads the magazine Grassroots Motorsports, you'll know that the magazine puts a lot of focus on autocrossing and great handling cars... check out <A HREF="http://www.grmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.grmotorsports.com</A>  and see for yourself.  The only problem is, there's apparently only been ONE article reguarding the Fiero in the couple of years that the magazine has been printed. You've got Miatas, RX7's, Porsches, mustangs, camaro's, vettes, and plenty of others.  But I ask you, where are the Fiero's?  I sent a letter to the Managing Editor about this and he sent a reply saying that the Fiero seems to have "lost popularity."  Please help me to convince him otherwise(even if you don't read the mag!)  We need input from those in the SCCA especially!  the address is: GRMDAVID@aol.com<P>WE MUST SAVE OUR PLASTIC FRIEND FROM BEING IGNORED!<P>peace,<BR>Geoff<P>||||reg||4
Z||000001||86SE||02-09-2000||09:55 PM||||I emailed that guy tellin him he should put some Fieros in his magazine!!||||reg||14
Z||000002||ITA_FIERO||02-09-2000||11:10 PM||||If we can get more guys to run their Fiero's in SOLO II, we would certainly get more coverage.  If the Fiero has "lost popularity" in SOLO II, there is probably a reason and it's up to us to get out there and show 'em these little cars can still run!  Overall, Fiero's still seem to fair well in SOLO competitions and you do see some in the race reports.  They are virtually extinct in road racing with only 4 or 5 running across the several regions I keep track of.  It's a shame, and I wish we could unite and put some American cars back on the podium!||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||02-10-2000||01:47 AM||||That's the spirit!!!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||turbo86se||02-10-2000||09:24 AM||||I'll send an email to him!!  The guys at our local track, Myrtle Beach Speedway, always cringe when a Fiero shows up. In fact, I don't think they're allowed on the track. They claim they handle too well. One day this summer, I'll have to see for myself!  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Greg Piet||02-10-2000||03:23 PM||||I know at the short track at Las vegas motor speedway, there is a class of "older" cars where it is limited to v6 or v8 american production cars made before 1990(or around there)and after 1970(or around there), and the engine must be in the front of the car.  Hearing this it bothered me, because the engine rule excludes Fiero's exclusively I believe.  I dont know of any other rear engine american cars that would fall in this category.  Because of this I figured they might as well change the engine rule wording to "NO FIERO'S!".||||reg||1
